  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 217 AM CDT Tue Sep 29 2026\n\nEarly this morning, radar and satellite imagery shows the\nongoing band of moderate to pockets of heavy rainfall over the\nRed River Valley terminating into far north-cental Minnesota.\nThis band will slowly move eastward over the rest of the\nNorthland later today and bring a tenth to a half-inch of \nrain. Once this band exits inland northwest Wisconsin early \ntonight, the trends for rainfall on Wednesday rainfall have \ntrended downward notably over the last 24 hours. What was once \nlooking like two different rounds of rainfall for northwest\nWisconsin is now more of just a round one event through this \nevening. Tonight, fog may develop across much of the region and\nwe will monitor for any Dense Fog Advisory needs if it looks to\nbe trending more dense over the next few forecast updates.\n\nThe fog tonight into Wednesday clears later Wednesday morning\nas daytime temperatures quickly spike and peak in the low to \nmid-70s, and a few spots in northwest Wisconsin may peak into \nthe upper-70s. Any lingering precipitation will be limited to \nspotty and light rain showers for inland northwest Wisconsin. \nThis warmth quickly passes though with a cold frontal passage \nearly Thursday morning.\n\nA cool down of 10 to 15 degrees will occur on Thursday compared to\nWednesday, but temperatures will still remain fairly seasonable\ngiven the very warm day Wednesday. Expect breezy conditions Thursday\nas winds gust 20 to 30 mph. A generally drier airmass sets up behind\nthe front, so dewpoints will be much lower into the rest of the\nforecast period, dropping into the 30s and 40s versus the 50s and\nlow-60s observed recently. We will expect much lower relative\nhumidity each day from Thursday onward.\n\nHigh pressure will dominate the late week and early next week \nweather pattern. From Thursday night onward, there is potential\neach night for frost somewhere in the region. Minimum \ntemperatures for Thursday night into early Friday morning have \ntrended downward, and frost or freeze headlines are very likely \nThursday night and maybe each night into the weekend for at \nleast some parts of the area.\n\nFriday night a Northern U.S. Rockies shortwave trough with \nPacific moisture deepens along the MT/ND/MB border area and \nhelps to create scattered light rain showers for Saturday \ndaytime as it passes through the Upper Midwest. Following this \nquick system, a generally minimal to no precipitation pattern is\nforecast into early next week.",
